[2026-02-04T18:07:23.940Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017oXrFigJ8WvjkXyZwLvfKL","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Trump IEEPA tariffs Federal Circuit Court Supreme Court 2026 unconstitutional\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"President Trump’s Tariffs and the Separation of Powers at the Supreme Court | Stanford Law School\",\"url\":\"https://law.stanford.edu/stanford-legal/president-trumps-tariffs-and-the-separation-of-powers-at-the-supreme-court/\"},{\"title\":\"Did the Supreme Court just signal the fate of Trump’s tariffs? | PIIE\",\"url\":\"https://www.piie.com/blogs/realtime-economics/2026/did-supreme-court-just-signal-fate-trumps-tariffs\"},{\"title\":\"Supreme Court tariff decision: U.S. Customs has new deadline for companies seeking refunds\",\"url\":\"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/08/supreme-court-trump-tariff-ruling-refunds.html\"},{\"title\":\"What’s at Stake in the Supreme Court Tariffs Case | Brennan Center for Justice\",\"url\":\"https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/whats-stake-supreme-court-tariffs-case\"},{\"title\":\"Supreme Court poised to decide fate of Trump’s emergency tariffs, a test of presidential power and a potential refund fight | Law Commentary\",\"url\":\"https://www.lawcommentary.com/articles/supreme-court-poised-to-decide-fate-of-trumps-emergency-tariffs-a-test-of-presidential-power-and-a-potential-refund-fight\"},{\"title\":\"Supreme Court showdown on tariffs shaping up as landmark case | Constitution Center\",\"url\":\"https://constitutioncenter.org/blog/supreme-court-showdown-on-tariffs-shaping-up-as-landmark-case\"},{\"title\":\"Update on IEEPA Tariff Refund Litigation: Where Things Stand and What Importers Should Consider Next | News & Events | Clark Hill PLC\",\"url\":\"https://www.clarkhill.com/news-events/news/update-on-ieepa-tariff-refund-litigation-where-things-stand-and-what-importers-should-consider-next/\"},{\"title\":\"Supreme Court Tariffs Case: Trump’s Options If IEEPA Levies Upheld as Illegal - Bloomberg\",\"url\":\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-08/supreme-court-tariffs-case-trump-s-options-if-ieepa-levies-upheld-as-illegal\"},{\"title\":\"Supreme Court Oral Arguments Signal Skepticism Toward Presidential Tariff Authority Under IEEPA | Insights | Holland & Knight\",\"url\":\"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2025/11/supreme-court-oral-arguments-signal-skepticism\"},{\"title\":\"The Supreme Court Takes Aim at Trump’s IEEPA Tariffs | Council on Foreign Relations\",\"url\":\"https://www.cfr.org/article/supreme-court-takes-aim-trumps-ieepa-tariffs\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding Trump's IEEPA tariffs and the legal challenges:\n\n## Federal Circuit Court Ruling\n\nIn a 7-4 ruling, the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit declared that the president's use of IEEPA tariffs was unlawful. The Federal Circuit affirmed a decision setting aside multiple executive orders that imposed tariffs \"of unlimited duration\" on imports from \"nearly every country,\" holding that IEEPA's authority to \"regulate\" imports did not amount to authority to impose tariffs.\n\n## Constitutional Questions Before the Supreme Court\n\nThe Supreme Court will decide if a specific act of Congress allows the president to impose tariffs, and if the act itself unconstitutionally delegates legislative authority to the president. The U.S. Constitution provides Congress with the exclusive authority to determine tariff levels. Presidents must rely on statutory authority provided by Congress to raise tariffs because they have no independent power to do so.\n\n## Supreme Court Timeline\n\nThe Supreme Court is expected to issue its decision in early 2026, although this timing is not guaranteed. The Court heard oral arguments in November 2025.\n\n## Key Legal Issues\n\nSeveral justices raised the \"major questions\" doctrine, noting that actions of vast economic and political significance require clear congressional authorization. They questioned whether the phrase \"regulate importation\" is sufficiently explicit to encompass the sweeping tariff authority claimed by the administration.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ffdb2836-5730-4184-8318-1c286009c5ec","uuid":"f2a652fa-ba20-415d-8f63-4d36c5d21d91","tool_use_result":{"query":"Trump IEEPA tariffs Federal Circuit Court Supreme Court 2026 unconstitutional","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Wk9GjmWmtvgFVUc6SAamGC","content":[{"title":"President Trump’s Tariffs and the Separation of Powers at the Supreme Court | Stanford Law School","url":"https://law.stanford.edu/stanford-legal/president-trumps-tariffs-and-the-separation-of-powers-at-the-supreme-court/"},{"title":"Did the Supreme Court just signal the fate of Trump’s tariffs? | PIIE","url":"https://www.piie.com/blogs/realtime-economics/2026/did-supreme-court-just-signal-fate-trumps-tariffs"},{"title":"Supreme Court tariff decision: U.S. Customs has new deadline for companies seeking refunds","url":"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/08/supreme-court-trump-tariff-ruling-refunds.html"},{"title":"What’s at Stake in the Supreme Court Tariffs Case | Brennan Center for Justice","url":"https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/whats-stake-supreme-court-tariffs-case"},{"title":"Supreme Court poised to decide fate of Trump’s emergency tariffs, a test of presidential power and a potential refund fight | Law Commentary","url":"https://www.lawcommentary.com/articles/supreme-court-poised-to-decide-fate-of-trumps-emergency-tariffs-a-test-of-presidential-power-and-a-potential-refund-fight"},{"title":"Supreme Court showdown on tariffs shaping up as landmark case | Constitution Center","url":"https://constitutioncenter.org/blog/supreme-court-showdown-on-tariffs-shaping-up-as-landmark-case"},{"title":"Update on IEEPA Tariff Refund Litigation: Where Things Stand and What Importers Should Consider Next | News & Events | Clark Hill PLC","url":"https://www.clarkhill.com/news-events/news/update-on-ieepa-tariff-refund-litigation-where-things-stand-and-what-importers-should-consider-next/"},{"title":"Supreme Court Tariffs Case: Trump’s Options If IEEPA Levies Upheld as Illegal - Bloomberg","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-08/supreme-court-tariffs-case-trump-s-options-if-ieepa-levies-upheld-as-illegal"},{"title":"Supreme Court Oral Arguments Signal Skepticism Toward Presidential Tariff Authority Under IEEPA | Insights | Holland & Knight","url":"https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2025/11/supreme-court-oral-arguments-signal-skepticism"},{"title":"The Supreme Court Takes Aim at Trump’s IEEPA Tariffs | Council on Foreign Relations","url":"https://www.cfr.org/article/supreme-court-takes-aim-trumps-ieepa-tariffs"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding Trump's IEEPA tariffs and the legal challenges:\n\n## Federal Circuit Court Ruling\n\nIn a 7-4 ruling, the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit declared that the president's use of IEEPA tariffs was unlawful. The Federal Circuit affirmed a decision setting aside multiple executive orders that imposed tariffs \"of unlimited duration\" on imports from \"nearly every country,\" holding that IEEPA's authority to \"regulate\" imports did not amount to authority to impose tariffs.\n\n## Constitutional Questions Before the Supreme Court\n\nThe Supreme Court will decide if a specific act of Congress allows the president to impose tariffs, and if the act itself unconstitutionally delegates legislative authority to the president. The U.S. Constitution provides Congress with the exclusive authority to determine tariff levels. Presidents must rely on statutory authority provided by Congress to raise tariffs because they have no independent power to do so.\n\n## Supreme Court Timeline\n\nThe Supreme Court is expected to issue its decision in early 2026, although this timing is not guaranteed. The Court heard oral arguments in November 2025.\n\n## Key Legal Issues\n\nSeveral justices raised the \"major questions\" doctrine, noting that actions of vast economic and political significance require clear congressional authorization. They questioned whether the phrase \"regulate importation\" is sufficiently explicit to encompass the sweeping tariff authority claimed by the administration."],"durationSeconds":6.749362700000002}}
